{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "5e493ea4-303c-423b-a9e7-cb1b49b58edc",
  "title": "Partial Borrows: The Borrow Checker's Oldest Paper Cut",
  "created_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nborrow = \"2\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": "#![allow(unused_imports)] // borrow::Partial derive emits a self-import that is unused when a cell does not touch the graph\n// Add ONE derive and the wall in Act 1 disappears. Everything else is the\n// same plain struct: two Vecs, a couple of helpers.\n#[derive(Debug, borrow::Partial)]\n#[module(crate::shared)]\npub struct Graph {\n    pub nodes: Vec<String>,\n    pub edges: Vec<(usize, usize)>,\n}\n\n/// A three-node starting point: A -> B -> C.\npub fn sample_graph() -> Graph {\n    Graph {\n        nodes: vec![\"A\".into(), \"B\".into(), \"C\".into()],\n        edges: vec![(0, 1), (1, 2)],\n    }\n}\n\n/// Render the graph as an SVG (nodes on a circle, edges as arrows).\npub fn graph_svg(g: &Graph, title: &str) -> String {\n    let n = g.nodes.len().max(1);\n    let (cx, cy, r) = (200.0_f64, 175.0_f64, 95.0_f64);\n    let pos: Vec<(f64, f64)> = (0..g.nodes.len())\n        .map(|i| {\n            let a = std::f64::consts::TAU * (i as f64) / (n as f64)\n                - std::f64::consts::FRAC_PI_2;\n            (cx + r * a.cos(), cy + r * a.sin())\n        })\n        .collect();\n\n    let mut edges_svg = String::new();\n    for &(from, to) in &g.edges {\n        if let (Some(&(x1, y1)), Some(&(x2, y2))) = (pos.get(from), pos.get(to)) {\n            edges_svg.push_str(&format!(\n                r##\"<line x1=\"{x1:.1}\" y1=\"{y1:.1}\" x2=\"{x2:.1}\" y2=\"{y2:.1}\" stroke=\"#8a8f98\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#a)\"/>\"##\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n\n    let mut nodes_svg = String::new();\n    for (i, label) in g.nodes.iter().enumerate() {\n        let (x, y) = pos[i];\n        nodes_svg.push_str(&format!(\n            r##\"<circle cx=\"{x:.1}\" cy=\"{y:.1}\" r=\"21\" fill=\"#ff5da2\" stroke=\"#12121e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"{x:.1}\" y=\"{y:.1}\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" fill=\"#12121e\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"700\" font-size=\"15\">{label}</text>\"##\n        ));\n    }\n\n    format!(\n        r##\"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 360\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:400px;background:#12121e;border-radius:10px;display:block;\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"a\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"20\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#8a8f98\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#e6e6f0\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">{title}</text>\n  {edges_svg}\n  {nodes_svg}\n</svg>\"##\n    )\n}\n",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "The oldest paper cut",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Partial borrows: the borrow checker's oldest paper cut\n\nEvery Rust developer has personally lost this fight. You have a struct, it has two fields, and you want to hand one method the first field and another method the second field, mutably, at the same time. The compiler says no, some variant of:\n\n> error[E0499]: cannot borrow `*self` as mutable more than once at a time\n\nYou *know* the two methods touch disjoint fields. The borrow checker does not, because it never looks inside a method to find out. On a slice you get a pressure valve: [`split_at_mut`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.slice.html#method.split_at_mut) hands you two disjoint `&mut` halves and everyone goes home happy. On the *fields of a struct*, through methods, there has never been an equivalent. This is one of the oldest **paper cuts** in the language.\n\nHere is the fun part: in 2026 this paper cut finally has **three answers moving at once**.\n\n1. A crate, [`borrow` v2](https://lib.rs/crates/borrow), that ships the future syntax *today*, on stable.\n2. [Niko Matsakis' \"maximally minimal view types\"](https://smallcultfollowing.com/babysteps/blog/2026/03/21/view-types-max-min/) (March 21, 2026), the language-side proposal.\n3. The [Reborrow traits 2026 project goal](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/reborrow-traits.html), already landing on nightly for the easy cases.\n\nWe are going to hit the wall on purpose, watch a crate walk straight through it, and read the tea leaves on which of the three lands first. There is a Polonius sidebar at the end too, because the *other* great borrow-checker modernization deserves a cameo.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Act 1: the wall",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Act 1: the wall, live\n\nBelow is a `Graph` with two fields, `nodes` and `edges`, and two methods that each borrow exactly one of them. We grab a mutable handle to each, back to back, and try to use both. **This cell does not compile, and that is the whole point.** ironpad renders the compiler's diagnostic inline, right where rustc points.\n\n(Our borrow here is a local we named `g` rather than `self`, so the headline names `g` instead of `*self`: **cannot borrow g as mutable more than once**. Same E0499, same lesson.)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "The wall (does not compile)",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// ironpad-test: compile-fail (this cell is a deliberate teaching failure)\nstruct Graph {\n    nodes: Vec<String>,\n    edges: Vec<(usize, usize)>,\n}\n\nimpl Graph {\n    fn nodes_mut(&mut self) -> &mut Vec<String> {\n        &mut self.nodes\n    }\n    fn edges_mut(&mut self) -> &mut Vec<(usize, usize)> {\n        &mut self.edges\n    }\n}\n\nlet mut g = Graph {\n    nodes: vec![\"A\".into(), \"B\".into()],\n    edges: vec![(0, 1)],\n};\n\n// Grab a mutable handle to each field, one method at a time...\nlet ns = g.nodes_mut();\nlet es = g.edges_mut(); // the compiler stops right here.\n\nns.push(\"C\".into());\nes.push((1, 2));\n\nCellOutput::text(\"unreachable: this cell never compiles\")\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "The signature is the contract",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The signature is the contract\n\nRead `nodes_mut` again: `fn nodes_mut(&mut self) -> &mut Vec<String>`. At the call site, that signature is *all the compiler is allowed to know*. It says \"I borrow **all of `self`** mutably, and I hand you back a reference tied to that borrow.\" The body clearly only touches `self.nodes`, but the borrow checker refuses to read the body to find that out, and for good reason. If it did, every caller would be coupled to the *implementation* of every method, and changing a method body could break code three crates away. **Signatures are the operative boundary.** The checker reasons about the signature, and nothing past it.\n\nThat is also exactly why [`split_at_mut`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.slice.html#method.split_at_mut) works and your two methods do not. Its signature *promises* disjointness right in the return type, `(&mut [T], &mut [T])`, two independent borrows spelled out where the caller can see them. So the whole game is to give an ordinary struct a way to say that same thing in a signature. That is what all three of 2026's answers are really about.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_4",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "Act 2: ship the future syntax",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Act 2: a crate that ships the future syntax\n\nThe [`borrow` crate](https://lib.rs/crates/borrow) (v2, October 2025) does something delightful: it lets you write a partial borrow *in the type* today, on stable Rust, with no nightly flag. You add `#[derive(borrow::Partial)]` to the struct, and then a function can ask for exactly the fields it needs:\n\n- `p!(&<mut nodes> Graph)` means \"a `Graph` whose `nodes` I may mutate, and nothing else.\"\n- `p!(&<mut edges> Graph)` means the same for `edges`.\n\nTwo functions with those two signatures can run against the same graph without a fight, because each one's *signature* now names its footprint, exactly the trick `split_at_mut` pulls. The `Graph` in this notebook's shared source carries the derive. Watch the two functions coexist.\n\n*(The kicker, and the reason this is worth your attention: `p!(&<mut nodes> Graph)` is roughly the **shape** the language feature might eventually take. This is a crate letting you rehearse a syntax that is still being designed.)*",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_5",
      "order": 5,
      "label": "Partial borrows, side by side",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use borrow::partial as p;\nuse borrow::traits::*;\nuse shared::Graph;\n\n// Each function names EXACTLY the field it touches, and nothing else.\nfn add_node(g: p!(&<mut nodes> Graph), label: &str) {\n    g.nodes.push(label.to_string());\n}\nfn add_edge(g: p!(&<mut edges> Graph), from: usize, to: usize) {\n    g.edges.push((from, to));\n}\n\nlet mut g = shared::sample_graph();\n\n// Two \"&mut self through a method\" calls the checker is perfectly happy with.\nadd_node(p!(&mut g), \"D\");\nadd_edge(p!(&mut g), 2, 3);\n\nlet svg = shared::graph_svg(&g, \"add_node + add_edge, no fight\");\n(\n    Svg(svg),\n    Md(format!(\n        \"Now **{} nodes** and **{} edges**. Two mutations, zero `E0499`.\",\n        g.nodes.len(),\n        g.edges.len()\n    )),\n)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_6",
      "order": 6,
      "label": "Were they alive at once?",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## But were they ever alive at the same time?\n\nFair challenge. In the cell above, `add_node` finished completely before `add_edge` started, so a skeptic could argue the two borrows never actually overlapped. So let us make them overlap. The `borrow` crate can split one borrow into two disjoint, *simultaneously live* mutable views, the struct-field analogue of `split_at_mut`. Below, `nodes` and `rest` are both alive on the same lines, both mutable, pointed at different fields. **No E0499.**",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_7",
      "order": 7,
      "label": "Two live &mut borrows, one struct",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use borrow::partial as p;\nuse borrow::traits::*;\nuse shared::Graph;\n\nlet mut g = shared::sample_graph();\n\n// Split ONE borrow into two disjoint, simultaneously-live mutable views.\nlet whole: p!(&<mut *> Graph) = p!(&mut g);\nlet (mut nodes, mut rest) = whole.borrow_nodes_mut();\n// `nodes` reaches only the node list; `rest` reaches only the edges.\n\nnodes.push(\"D\".into());\nrest.edges.push((2, 3));\n// Two mutable borrows of one struct, live at the same instant. Legal.\n\nlet svg = shared::graph_svg(&g, \"two live &mut borrows, one struct\");\nSvg(svg)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_8",
      "order": 8,
      "label": "Act 3: the language answers",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Act 3: what the language is actually building\n\nThe crate is a rehearsal. Here is the real cast.\n\n**[Maximally minimal view types](https://smallcultfollowing.com/babysteps/blog/2026/03/21/view-types-max-min/) (Niko Matsakis, March 21, 2026).** A deliberately *stripped-down* take on letting a function's signature name the fields it touches, something like `fn add_node(&mut self.nodes)`. The \"maximally minimal\" framing is the whole point: earlier view-type proposals tried to do everything and stalled, so this one tries to do the *least* that still solves the disjoint-borrow case, on the theory that a small feature that ships beats a general one that does not.\n\n**[Reborrow and CoerceShared traits](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/reborrow-traits.html) (2026 project goal).** A different attack: give the language first-class `Reborrow` and `CoerceShared` traits so user types can opt into the same reborrowing magic that `&mut T` gets for free. The 2025H2 update reported a **working nightly implementation for types with a single lifetime and trivial layout**, now iterating on feedback. It is the furthest along in actual compiler code.\n\nOne honest note before you conflate things. Partial borrows are **specifically distinct from** field projections, the other \"reach into a struct\" feature people are excited about. Projection is about turning a `Pin<&mut Wrapper>` into a `Pin<&mut Field>`, projecting a wrapper through to one of its fields. Partial borrows are about holding *several disjoint field borrows at once*. Adjacent neighborhoods, different houses; do not let anyone tell you one subsumes the other.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_9",
      "order": 9,
      "label": "Sidebar: Polonius",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Sidebar: Polonius, the other modernization\n\nPartial borrows are about teaching the checker to see *disjoint fields*. [Polonius](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/polonius.html) is about teaching it to see *time* more precisely. It is a from-scratch reformulation of the borrow checker that is **location-sensitive**: it tracks which loans are live at each point in the control-flow graph, instead of approximating with the coarser regions today's NLL checker uses.\n\nThe cleanest way to feel the difference is the canonical case NLL gets wrong, known affectionately as **\"NLL problem case 3.\"** Return a borrow on one branch, mutate on the other. You and I can both see the borrow from the early return cannot possibly be alive on the line that mutates, because the early return already left the function. Today's checker cannot see it. Watch.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_10",
      "order": 10,
      "label": "NLL problem case 3 (does not compile)",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// ironpad-test: compile-fail (this cell is a deliberate teaching failure)\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\n// NLL Problem Case 3: return a borrow on one path, mutate on the other.\nfn get_or_insert(map: &mut HashMap<u32, String>, key: u32) -> &String {\n    if let Some(value) = map.get(&key) {\n        return value; // borrow of `map` \"escapes\" through this return...\n    }\n    map.insert(key, \"default\".to_string()); // ...and collides right here.\n    &map[&key]\n}\n\nlet mut m = HashMap::new();\nm.insert(1, \"one\".to_string());\nCellOutput::text(get_or_insert(&mut m, 1).clone())\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_11",
      "order": 11,
      "label": "What Polonius sees",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## What Polonius sees that NLL cannot\n\nThe error lands on `map.insert`, and the reason it gives is the tell:\n\n> returning this value requires that `*map` is borrowed for `'1`\n\nBecause `get` can return a reference that escapes through `return value`, today's checker conservatively assumes the borrow from `get` lasts for the *entire* rest of the function, including the `else` path where you know it is already gone. So the later `map.insert` looks like a conflict and you get **E0502**. Polonius, being location-sensitive, sees that on the `else` path no loan from `get` is live, and **accepts this code unchanged**. Same source, no rewrite, no `entry`-API gymnastics.\n\nThe status is genuinely exciting. On [February 28, 2026, rust#150551 landed and the driver called Polonius \"stabilizable\"](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/150551). The remaining cost is performance: the location-sensitive analysis is more expensive, and the team has signaled it is willing to **eat a 10 to 20% borrow-check hit** in the typical case (worst case near 60% on a pathological function) to make code like the cell above *just work*. That is a remarkable trade to put on the table, and a good measure of how much this one paper cut has cost the ecosystem in aggregate.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_12",
      "order": 12,
      "label": "Which lands first?",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Which one lands first? (a friendly wager)\n\nThree answers, one paper cut. Here is my bet, offered as *directional rather than precise*.\n\n**Polonius ships first**, and it is not that close. It is a `-Z` flag today, it has a landed PR its own driver calls \"stabilizable,\" and it needs no new surface syntax, which is where language features go to spend two extra years bikeshedding. Second, **Reborrow traits**, because there is real nightly code and the single-lifetime case already works; traits are a smaller ask than new borrow syntax. Third, and I say this with affection, **view types**, the feature with the best story and the longest road, because \"name a subset of your fields in a signature\" is exactly the kind of small-looking syntax that turns out to touch everything.\n\nAnd a fourth thing quietly wins in the meantime: the [`borrow` crate](https://lib.rs/crates/borrow) is *usable this afternoon*. That is the recurring lesson of the modern Rust ecosystem. When the language takes the scenic route, someone ships a crate that fakes the destination well enough to use in production, and by the time the real feature arrives half of us have forgotten we were ever waiting.\n\nPrimary sources, if you want them: [view types](https://smallcultfollowing.com/babysteps/blog/2026/03/21/view-types-max-min/), the [Reborrow traits goal](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/reborrow-traits.html), the [Polonius goal](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/polonius.html), and the [internals thread on partial borrows](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/notes-on-partial-borrows/20020) that started much of it.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "The borrow checker's oldest paper cut: why you still cannot mutably borrow two disjoint struct fields through methods, the `borrow` crate that ships the fix today on stable, and 2026's three language-side answers (view types, Reborrow traits, Polonius). Two cells fail to compile on purpose.",
  "tags": [
    "borrow-checker",
    "partial-borrows",
    "view-types",
    "polonius",
    "rust-2026"
  ]
}